= The health system and societal costs of osteoarthritis in New Zealand: Analysis plan
#smallcaps[Published]\ 06 May 2022

#smallcaps[Citation]\ #eval("Wilson R, Abbott JH, Pryymachenko Y, Schofield S, Shrestha R. The health system and societal costs of osteoarthritis in New Zealand: Analysis plan. FigShare, 06 May 2022. doi:~#link(\"https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.16900156\")[10.6084/m9.figshare.16900156]", mode: "markup")

=== Abstract
Osteoarthritis (OA) is among the most common chronic health conditions,
causing a significant health and social burden. OA is typically diagnosed in primary
care, and managed by general practitioners (GPs) during its clinical course for
several years until, in many cases, need for eventual referral to an orthopaedic
surgeon. Anecdotally, and in international data, it is recognised that management
of OA falls well short of best practice; however, little systematic information
is available on the burden or management of OA in primary care in New Zealand.

This study forms part of a larger research project, funded by the Health Research
Council of New Zealand (Project grant #18-442), to investigate the primary care
management of OA in New Zealand, the health and socio-economic costs of OA across
the whole course of the disease, and the potential gains from improving the delivery
of care to meet best-practice standards.

In this study, we will use osteoarthritis
(OA) diagnosis data from primary care (extracted from patient records in the first
phase of the project) and existing linked administrative data from the Ministry
of Health and other government departments and agencies to investigate the health
system and societal costs associated with knee and hip OA.

This analysis plan details the data sources and variables, statistical
methods, and outputs for this research.

